<sn><md>### Balanced Diet

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-md-9
Both **over-eating** and **under-eating** are `harmful` to health; while **consuming too much** `leads to obesity`—a major concern `in developed countries`—eating too little causes nutritional deficiencies. 

Maintaining good health requires consuming a balanced diet containing all `seven key food elements` in proper proportions to provide necessary calories and fiber for daily activities.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-md-3
![img](https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/MoMqkJw8nkKtdN7K6wP2Zn-600-80.jpg)
:::

:::


**Daily Caloric Requirements**

* Active Adult ++Male++: `~2,500` kilocalories
* Average Adult ++Female++: `~2,000` kilocalories
</md></sn><sn><md>### Dietary Considerations by Demographic

* **Age, Sex &amp; Activity Level:** Must be `factored` in when planning daily meals.
* **Babies &amp; Elderly:** Require `easily digestible`, low-fat foods.
* **Growing Children:** Need higher amounts of `protein, calcium, and phosphorus` for bone and tooth development.
* **Pregnant Women:** Require additional `protein, calcium, and iodine` for blood production and proper fetal development.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Preparation of Balanced Food

Because no single natural food fulfills all dietary requirements, a balanced diet must be planned using various food groups:

| Carbohydrate | Protein | Fat | Vitamin | Mineral |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Rice | Fish | Butter | Milk, Eggs | Milk |
| Wheat | Meat | Oil | Fruit | Eggs |
| Sugar / Molasses | Eggs | Ghee | Fish / Meat | Vegetables |</md></sn>